Feature: Extended Assertion Matrix
  Testing the complete set of output and file assertions
  Image: alpine:latest

  Scenario: stdout matches pattern
    When I run "echo 'version 1.2.3'"
    Then stdout matches pattern "version \d+\.\d+\.\d+"

  Scenario: stderr matches pattern
    When I run "sh -c 'echo error: boom >&2'"
    Then stderr matches pattern "error: \w+"

  Scenario: stderr does not match pattern
    When I run "echo hello"
    Then stderr does not match pattern "panic:"

  Scenario: output equals
    When I run "echo -n hello"
    Then output equals "hello"

  Scenario: stdout equals with trimming
    When I run "echo hello"
    Then stdout equals "hello"

  Scenario: stderr equals
    When I run "sh -c 'echo -n err >&2'"
    Then stderr equals "err"

  Scenario: output is empty
    When I run "true"
    Then output is empty

  Scenario: stderr is empty
    When I run "echo hello"
    Then stderr is empty

  Scenario: stderr is not empty
    When I run "sh -c 'echo something >&2'"
    Then stderr is not empty

  Scenario: file matches pattern
    Given a file "VERSION" with content:
      1.3.0
    When I run "cat VERSION"
    Then file "VERSION" matches pattern "^\d+\.\d+\.\d+$"

  Scenario: file does not match pattern
    Given a file "notes.txt" with content:
      everything is fine
    When I run "cat notes.txt"
    Then file "notes.txt" does not match pattern "^ERROR"

  Scenario: file equals
    Given a file "VERSION" with content:
      1.3.0
    When I run "cat VERSION"
    Then file "VERSION" equals "1.3.0"

  Scenario: file does not equal
    Given a file "VERSION" with content:
      1.3.0
    When I run "cat VERSION"
    Then file "VERSION" does not equal "2.0.0"

  Scenario: file is empty
    Given a file "empty.txt" with content:

    When I run "cat empty.txt"
    Then file "empty.txt" is empty

  Scenario: file is not empty
    Given a file "data.txt" with content:
      some data here
    When I run "cat data.txt"
    Then file "data.txt" is not empty
